WHY ZERO-CLAUSE, AND WHAT IT COVERS
-----------------------------------

A permissive licence grants what the repository CONTAINS, so the question this
answers is whether anything in the tree is not the author's to give away. It is
not: 185 tracked files, all first-party, and this repository is one of the two
in the portfolio that is public -- swept for drawing files, exports, archives
and customer names and measured clean.

What this repository READS is a customer's drawing set. What it CARRIES is
none: no PDF, no DXF, no exported wire list, no project. The wire-number parser
encodes a naming convention rather than a transcribed vendor publication, which
is the line that keeps this side of the portfolio's proprietary boundary.

Zero-clause rather than MIT because MIT REQUIRES ATTRIBUTION from every
redistributor, and that is a condition nobody here wants to impose. 0BSD is a
normal licence grant with every condition removed -- and it is a grant rather
than a public-domain dedication, because several jurisdictions do not let an
author abandon copyright and a dedication then leans on a fallback clause.


THE DESIGN-RULE HALF IS A SEPARATE, PROPRIETARY COMPONENT
---------------------------------------------------------

`requirements-drc.txt` installs PyDRC from a private repository, and PyDRC is
licensed for use inside DSI Innovations, LLC and not for redistribution outside
it. It is NOT in this tree and this grant does not reach it. Somebody taking
DSI Redline under the terms above gets the reviewer and not the audit: the app
reports the design-rule tab unavailable rather than breaking, which is what
`tools/run_tests.py`'s skip banners and CI's `--require-drc` flag are about.


THE BUNDLED DEPENDENCIES ARE NOT COVERED, AND ONE OF THEM IS UNRESOLVED
-----------------------------------------------------------------------

`THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md` and `licenses/` beside it are the record, and they are
installed with the app rather than left at the repository root -- MIT, ISC and
the BSD licences require the notice to travel with the DISTRIBUTED form, and a
file in a repository satisfies nothing for somebody who downloads the `.exe`.

Read that file before redistributing a build. It records, in its own words, that
PyMuPDF declares `Dual Licensed - GNU AFFERO GPL 3.0 or Artifex Commercial
License`, that it is bundled inside the shipped installer, and that WHICH OF THE
TWO ARMS APPLIES HAS NOT BEEN ESTABLISHED. Nothing in this file settles that:
this grant is about the author's own code and cannot license somebody else's.
